AWS-003 — Canonical Workflow Object (CWO)

Document ID: AWS-003
Title: Canonical Workflow Object (CWO)
Version: 1.0.0-draft
Status: Draft

1. Purpose

This specification defines the Canonical Workflow Object (CWO), the standardized representation of executable workflows within the Atlas Engineering Cognition Framework (AECF).

The CWO establishes a governed, versioned, traceable, interoperable, and deterministic workflow model capable of orchestrating engineering activities, decisions, participants, systems, and AI agents while preserving engineering intent.

2. Scope

This specification defines:

Workflow architecture
Workflow structure
Activities
States
Transitions
Participants
Inputs and outputs
Governance
Relationships

This specification applies to every workflow defined within Atlas.

3. Canonical Definition

A Canonical Workflow Object (CWO) is a specialization of the Canonical Atlas Object that formally represents a governed sequence of engineering activities, decisions, transitions, and interactions performed to achieve a defined engineering objective.

Workflows define execution.

They do not represent execution history.

4. Objectives

The Canonical Workflow Object SHALL:

Standardize workflow representation.
Preserve engineering intent.
Enable deterministic execution.
Support explainability.
Enable orchestration.
Support interoperability.
Preserve governance.
Maintain traceability.
5. Inheritance

The Canonical Workflow Object SHALL inherit every mandatory property of the Canonical Atlas Object.

Canonical Atlas Object
        │
        ▼
Canonical Workflow Object
        │
 ┌──────┼──────────────┐
 ▼      ▼              ▼
Design  Approval   Construction
Workflow Workflow    Workflow

Future workflow families SHALL extend this hierarchy.

6. Canonical Workflow Structure

Every CWO SHALL define:

Property	Description
Workflow ID	Globally unique identifier
Workflow Name	Human-readable name
Workflow Type	Canonical classification
Workflow Version	Version identifier
Workflow Status	Lifecycle state
Workflow Owner	Governance owner
Workflow Objective	Intended engineering outcome
Workflow Scope	Applicability boundary
7. Workflow Activities

Every workflow SHALL define one or more activities.

Each activity SHALL include:

Activity identifier
Activity name
Activity type
Preconditions
Postconditions
Required inputs
Produced outputs
Responsible participant
Expected outcome

Activities SHALL be independently identifiable.

8. Workflow States

Every workflow SHALL define explicit execution states.

Atlas defines the following canonical workflow states.

State	Description
Initialized	Workflow created
Ready	Awaiting execution
Executing	Activities in progress
Waiting	Awaiting event or participant
Suspended	Temporarily paused
Completed	Successfully finished
Failed	Execution unsuccessful
Cancelled	Terminated intentionally

Additional states MAY be introduced through workflow profiles.

9. Workflow Transitions

Every workflow SHALL explicitly define permitted state transitions.

Each transition SHALL specify:

Source state
Target state
Trigger
Transition conditions
Responsible authority
Generated events

Transitions SHALL be deterministic.

10. Workflow Participants

Every workflow SHALL define participating entities.

Participants MAY include:

Human engineers
Reviewers
Organizations
External systems
AI agents
Digital Twins
Automated services

Every participant SHALL define:

Identity
Role
Responsibilities
Authority level
Trust requirements
11. Workflow Inputs

Every workflow SHALL explicitly define its required inputs.

Examples include:

Canonical Atlas Objects
Engineering models
Requirements
Decisions
Policies
Events
External data

Inputs SHALL remain versioned and traceable.

12. Workflow Outputs

Every workflow SHALL define produced outputs.

Examples include:

Engineering artifacts
Decision Records
Policy Decisions
Validation Results
Reports
Canonical Events
Notifications
Digital Twin updates

Outputs SHALL conform to applicable Atlas specifications.

13. Workflow Relationships

Workflows MAY define relationships including:

Relationship	Purpose
Depends On	Requires another workflow
Invokes	Starts another workflow
Extends	Adds workflow behavior
References	Uses another workflow
Produces	Creates governed artifacts
Consumes	Uses governed artifacts

Relationships SHALL conform to ADS-011 — Relationship Model.

14. Workflow Metadata

Every workflow SHALL include:

Canonical metadata
Governance metadata
Version information
Lifecycle state
Provenance
Audit references
Compliance references

Metadata SHALL conform to ADS-010 — Metadata Model.

15. Workflow Governance

Every workflow SHALL define:

Workflow owner
Steward
Execution authority
Review authority
Applicable policies
Governance domain
Audit requirements

Governance SHALL remain attached throughout the workflow lifecycle.

16. Workflow Quality

Every workflow SHALL satisfy:

Correctness
Completeness
Determinism
Explainability
Traceability
Maintainability
Scalability
Auditability

Quality SHALL be measurable.

17. Conformance

A Canonical Workflow Object conforms if it:

Inherits the CAO contract.
Defines mandatory workflow properties.
Specifies activities.
Defines states and transitions.
Declares participants.
Defines inputs and outputs.
Supports governance.
Maintains traceability.
18. Future Evolution

Future revisions MAY introduce:

Adaptive workflow definitions.
AI-generated workflow templates.
Semantic workflow discovery.
Digital Twin workflow profiles.
Cross-enterprise workflow libraries.
Domain-specific workflow extensions.

Extensions SHALL preserve canonical workflow semantics.

